![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The current implementation of `relu2_kernel` does not propagate `NaN` values, as `f > 0.0f` evaluates to false for `NaN`, resulting in `0.0f`. While this matches the behavior of some other kernels in this file (like `fatrelu_kernel`), it deviates from PyTorch's `F.relu` and `torch.square` behavior, which propagate `NaN`. Consider using a ternary that explicitly handles `NaN` if strict parity with PyTorch is required.

```
template <typename T>
__device__ __forceinline__ T relu2_kernel(const T& x) {
  const float f = (float)x;
  // Propagate NaN to match PyTorch behavior
  if (isnan(f)) return (T)f;
  const float r = f > 0.0f ? f : 0.0f;
  return (T)(r * r);
}
```

